<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kim Jong Il</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/2011">2011</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kim Jong Il was a North Korean politician and dictator who was the second supreme leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> from the death of his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a>, in 1994 until his own death in 2011. Born in the far east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, he rose through the Workers' Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, helped develop the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juche">Juche</a> ideology and the cult of personality, and was designated his father's successor in 1980. He took power amid a catastrophic economic crisis and famine, ruled a repressive and totalitarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> and made the army central through his Songun (military-first) policies. North Korea conducted its first nuclear test in 2006 under his rule; he promoted his third son, Kim Jong Un, as his successor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kim-jong-il.jpg" alt="Kim Jong Il" width="330" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jesse Charlie · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kim_Jong_il_Portrait-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Kim Jong Il was born on 16 February 1941 in the far east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, generally agreed to be the camp of Voroshilov near Nikolsk. His mother, Kim Jong-suk, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a>'s first wife, and the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> in 1945. He joined the Workers' Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in July 1961 and its Propaganda and Agitation Department in 1966.</p><p>After the Kapsan faction incident around 1967, the last credible challenge to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a>'s sole rule, he took part in the investigation and purges that followed, his first official duties. He became director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> in 1973 and was formally designated his father's successor in October 1980 at the party congress, taking the title Dear Leader as a personality cult was built around him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a> died on 8 July 1994 and Kim Jong Il became supreme leader. The economy struggled through the 1990s because of mismanagement and severe floods, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> could not import enough to sustain industry, which brought a severe famine. He adopted the Songun, military-first, policy, making the army the central organiser of civil society. In 1996 he launched a purge named the Deepening Group Incident; some 25,000 people are thought to have been purged, about 10,000 of them executed.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>'s Sunshine Policy from 1998 led to Kim's meeting with Kim Dae-jung in 2000 and to the Kaesong Industrial Park in 2003, though the sinking of ROKS Cheonan and the bombardment of Yeonpyeong in 2010 ended it. In 2002 the government admitted producing nuclear weapons in violation of the 1994 Agreed Framework with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and it announced an underground nuclear test on 9 October 2006.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After he failed to appear at public events in 2008, observers assumed he was ill, and he began to promote his third son, Kim Jong Un, raising him to high party posts in 2010. Kim Jong Il died of a suspected heart attack on 17 December 2011 while travelling by train, and the government announced it two days later. His funeral took place on 28 December in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, and he was declared Eternal General Secretary of the party and Eternal Chairman of the National Defence Commission.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kim Jong Il” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Economic self-sufficiency (charip) is held to be required for political independence; in On the Juche Idea <b>Kim Jong Il</b> argued for an independent national economy based on heavy industry.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/juche">Juche · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 1980s <b>Kim Jong Il</b> sought to reduce the song's importance in favour of the &quot;Song of General Kim Il Sung&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/aegukka">Aegukka (North Korea)</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;No Motherland Without You&quot;, also called &quot;Ode to <b>Kim Jong Il</b>&quot;, is a North Korean song about the country's second supreme leader, Kim Jong Il.</blockquote><a href="/songs/no-motherland-without-you">No Motherland Without You</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 1980s <b>Kim Jong Il</b> began promoting the song, and it has since displaced &quot;Aegukka&quot;, the national anthem, as the most important song in the country and the de facto anthem played at public gatherings, and it has been sung more than the official anthem since…</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-general-kim-il-sung">Song of General Kim Il Sung</a></li><li><blockquote>It praises <b>Kim Jong Il</b>, who ruled from 1994 to 2011 and is styled the &quot;Eternal General Secretary&quot;, as part of his personality cult; under his father Kim Il Sung he had himself helped to run the cult of the elder Kim.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-general-kim-jong-il">Song of General Kim Jong Il</a></li><li><blockquote>Its words are attributed to <b>Kim Jong Il</b>, later the country's Supreme Leader; the Korean article describes him as its presumed composer.</blockquote><a href="/songs/where-are-you-dear-general">Where Are You, Dear General?</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vyatskoye, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.74,135.74&amp;year=1986" aria-label="Vyatskoye on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Kim Jong Il"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4204 5871h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4204 5871h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.74,135.74&amp;year=1986">What was here in 1986? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
